  <acceptance-criteria testable="true">
    <criterion testable="true">Every editable paragraph in the corpus docs resolves to a unique `w14:paraId` that is PRESENT IN THE RETAINED PACKAGE BYTES (not only in the projection map), including paragraphs inside table cells and nested tables.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Ids survive a load → save → reload round-trip (the same paragraph resolves to the same paraId after reload).</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Negative: a document that already carries `w14:paraId` values on its paragraphs is NOT re-minted — existing ids are left byte-identical (idempotent).</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">Ids are written via OpenXml DOM mutation, never by string-editing `document.xml`.</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">`Services/Compose/` injects no AI-internal type (Tier-1 NetArchTest green); no `Microsoft.Graph` type in `Services/Compose/` (ADR-007).</criterion>
    <criterion testable="true">BFF builds green; publish size reported absolute + delta vs ~49.63 MB baseline, ≤60 MB; no new HIGH CVE.</criterion>
  </acceptance-criteria>

  <notes>
    Extends a KEEP-list asset — no `&lt;justification&gt;` block (modify-only, not new surface). The through-the-wire
    proof that persisted ids survive ingest lives in task 013's seam slice. Coordinate the `Services/Compose/`
    cutover with `spaarkeai-compose-r3` (deployed base).

    COMPLETED (2026-07-22). Added `ComposeBaselineParaIdStamper.MintAndPersist(ReadOnlyMemory&lt;byte&gt; source)` —
    reuses `ParaIdPreParser.Parse` (read-only pass) to decide which paragraphs need a mint, then opens the SAME
    bytes editable and writes each minted id into the DOM (`w14:paraId` attribute), never string-editing
    `document.xml`. Idempotent by construction: when `ParaIdPreParser` finds no paragraph needing a mint the method
    returns before ever opening the package editable. Wired into `ComposeService.LoadAsync` immediately after the
    SPE download, BEFORE `ComposeDocxProjectionBuilder.Build` runs — so the returned `Content` bytes (the same
    bytes SaveAsync's fast path echoes back as the baseline) already carry persisted ids, and the projection
    builder's own map is naturally consistent (it finds ids already present). Did NOT touch
    `ComposeDocxProjectionBuilder.cs` / `ComposeDocxProjection.cs` (task 011 ownership, avoided per instruction).

    Self-caught bug during verification: the first cut read `buffer.ToArray()` *inside* the `using (doc)` block —
    before `WordprocessingDocument.Dispose()` flushes the OPC package's ZIP central directory — so the minted ids
    never actually reached the returned bytes. Fixed to match `Stamp()`'s proven structure (return
    `buffer.ToArray()` only after the `using` block closes). Caught by the new unit tests, not by inspection alone.

    Acceptance criteria verified:
    - Every editable paragraph across all 3 corpus fixtures resolves to a unique, non-empty paraId PHYSICALLY
      present in the returned bytes (test: `MintAndPersist_AcrossTheFidelityCorpus_...`, + `LoadAsync` wiring proof
      `LoadAsync_ForFormattedDocument_PersistsMintedParaIdsIntoTheReturnedContentBytes`).
    - Round-trip: applying `MintAndPersist` a second time to its own output is a no-op, ids byte-identical
      (`MintAndPersist_AppliedASecondTimeToItsOwnOutput_...`).
    - Negative/idempotency: a document whose paragraphs already all carry ids is returned byte-identical, never
      re-minted (`MintAndPersist_DocumentWhereEveryParagraphAlreadyHasAnId_...`).
    - DOM mutation only (OpenXml `ParagraphId` assignment), never string-edit.
    - Tier-1 NetArchTest (`ADR013_ComposeFacadeTests`) green; no `Microsoft.Graph` type (grep-verified, 0 matches).

    One PRE-EXISTING test's assertion updated (not a new behavior, a correctly-reasoned consequence of this task):
    `ComposeServiceLoadParaIdTests.LoadAsync_ForFormattedDocument_ProjectsCompleteParaIdMapOntoResponse` previously
    asserted `IsMinted == true` for id-less paragraphs in the response's `ParaIdMap`. Because minting now happens
    at ingest BEFORE the projection builder runs, the builder's own `IsMinted` flag reads `false` for those
    paragraphs (it finds ids already physically present). The still-load-bearing invariant — unique, persisted,
    non-empty ids — is unchanged and covered by the other assertions in that same test plus the new byte-level
    test. Updated inline with a comment explaining the semantic shift.

    Files changed: `src/server/api/Sprk.Bff.Api/Services/Compose/ComposeBaselineParaIdStamper.cs`,
    `src/server/api/Sprk.Bff.Api/Services/Compose/ComposeService.cs`,
    `tests/unit/Sprk.Bff.Api.Tests/Services/Compose/ComposeBaselineParaIdStamperTests.cs`,
    `tests/unit/Sprk.Bff.Api.Tests/Services/Compose/ComposeServiceLoadParaIdTests.cs`.

    Build: green (0 errors). Full `Services/Compose` unit suite: 293/293 passed. Seam suite (incl. the no-op
    round-trip byte-diff harness, unaffected since it exercises Save not Load): 3/3 passed. Publish size: 47.47 MB
    compressed incl. PDBs (measured via `dotnet publish -c Release` + `Compress-Archive`), vs the task-prompt-cited
    ~46.11 MB baseline — delta +1.36 MB, well under the +5 MB single-task-delta justification threshold and the
    55/60 MB escalation/hard-ceiling. Caveat: this worktree had task 011 (a parallel agent) concurrently modifying
    `ComposeDocxProjectionBuilder.cs`/`ComposeDocxProjection.cs` in the same publish output, so the delta is not
    cleanly attributable to this task's diff alone (no new `&lt;PackageReference&gt;` was added by this task —
    confirmed via `git diff --stat` on the `.csproj`, empty). CVE scan: one PRE-EXISTING HIGH-severity advisory on
    `System.Security.Cryptography.Xml` 8.0.3 (transitive) — not introduced by this task (no csproj change).
    Quality gates: code-review (APPROVED, 0 Critical/Warning, 1 non-blocking Suggestion) and adr-check (0
    violations, 0 warnings) both passed this session.
  </notes>

